abstract: >
  Preregistered Batch-API tests of prompt-injection resistance across three frontier
  models (Claude Opus 4.8, Sonnet 5, Haiku 4.5). Sonnet 5 detects a blatant injection on
  every audit probe while obeying it on 64 of 80 tries over 20 tasks (0.80, Wilson 95% CI
  0.70 to 0.87; tries within a task are not independent) when the injection is the last
  text in the prompt. Kariyappa and Suh (2026, arXiv:2606.07808) reported a similar gap
  first; this is an independent observation, not claimed as a discovery. The obey rate replicates across four
  independent same-prompt batches (0.738, 0.787, 0.825, 0.850; mean 0.800) but is
  contingent on the injection being the last text in the prompt: on a length ladder with
  wording held constant, trailing neutral text takes it to 0.263 at 100 characters and
  0.000 at 200, and the same position contrast moved nothing on two open-weight models.
  The contribution beyond replication is the presentation channel: the identical payload
  moves Sonnet 5 from 0.00 to 0.31 compliance (mean over 14 tasks) only when it is labelled
  as a system instruction, tool output or cited specification (+0.307, 90% CI 0.226 to
  0.390, n=14; below the lab's power bar), while the same model resists a
  fabricated reasoning technique (powered null). A block-labeling defense drove observed
  compliance to zero at n=14 (one-sided 95% upper bound about 19%); applied to a reviewing
  rather than an acting model it degraded discrimination (0.555 to 0.003 on a leak-free
  re-run), so it is scoped to the actor channel. Version 1.1.0 adds the reviewer side,
  measured on open-weight monitors (12B-35B, n=337 per cell, most results replicated on a
  second task pool). A monitor's recorded verdict can be the attacker's appended word. The
  untrusted-input label makes a monitor obey dictated verdicts it otherwise ignores.
  Score-mode and verdict-first formats fall to an automated payload search, and monitor
  diversity stops only attacks aimed at one monitor. The one defense that held, showing the
  monitor only the extracted integer, fails once an LLM does the extracting. Raw completions
  and per-item verdicts ship with stdlib-only re-derivation scripts (reproduce.py,
  joint_table.py, recency.py, monitor_label.py, oversight.py) that need no API key and no
  network. Code is MIT-licensed; the data are CC0.
